Open in first person with the concrete reason I started the work. Put the
system, model, hardware, or failure in the first paragraph. Land the measured
result, mystery, or stakes before the first part heading.

## Running it

State the prerequisites before the command.

```bash
the smallest command that reproduces the useful operation
```

Explain what the reader should observe, which image or version is pinned, and
which parts of the environment materially affect the result.

## Part I: Start with the real operation

### The smallest artifact

Show one actual tensor operation, request, trace, command, error, kernel call,
or configuration block.

```text
real input -> real operation -> real output
```

Explain every visible dimension, field, argument, and unit in ordinary
language. Do not begin with the general theory.

### A tiny version I can hold in my head

Shrink the operation without changing the relationship being explained.
State exactly what the toy version removes.

```text
toy input -> same mechanism -> toy output
```

Map each toy object back to the real system before adding the next abstraction.

## Part II: Follow the data into the system

### What physically happens next

Name the component that owns the data now, the representation it receives, and
the representation it produces. Separate the logical view from the packed,
sharded, fused, cached, or lowered physical view.

<!--
Place an architecture or data-flow figure only when it carries information the
prose cannot carry as cleanly. Precede it with the claim. Follow it with the
reading. The caption states the finding.
-->

### The implementation boundary

Show the smallest source slice that proves the mechanism. Deep-dive snippets
should disclose the load-bearing code, mark every elision, and avoid dumping a
whole file.

```text
minimal source slice
```

Say what was removed and what this code proves.

## Part III: The investigation

### The baseline and measurement contract

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| hardware | |
| software and image | |
| workload | |
| statistic | p50, p90, p99, mean, minimum, or another named statistic |
| samples | |
| warm-up | |
| measurement window | |
| correctness check | |

### What I expected

Write the actual hypothesis I had at the time and the evidence that made it
reasonable.

### What failed

Keep failed paths when they changed the model of the system. State the
measurement or trace that ruled each one out.

### The change that held

Describe one change, re-measure it, and read the evidence before moving to the
next bottleneck. Repeat this section shape when the investigation had several
independent fixes.

## Part IV: Results and limits

### What moved

Present the comparison with the same axes, units, environment, and statistic.
Performance work needs the distribution and sample count, not only an average.

### What did not move

Name the remaining bottleneck, negative result, unsupported mode, or scope
boundary. Separate what was measured from what is inferred.

## What I want to try next

Return to the concrete question from the opening. Name the next experiment,
implementation step, or measurement I would run. Do not end with a generic
summary.
